1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Embed or reference? Model the ticket page from how it is read and written

2
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:14,000
A diagram without a workload isn't a design. These five needs decide where events live.

3
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:20,000
One read returns the ticket and its events. An append is one atomic document write.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,200
But every event makes this one document bigger, without a bound.

5
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:28,700
Patterns 4 and 5 push back: unbounded growth, and every report digs through every ticket.

6
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:35,400
The ticket stores requester_id. Each event is its own document pointing back to ticket 1001.

7
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:37,700
Read 1: findOne ticket 1001

8
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:40,000
Read 2: findOne user_id = requester_id

9
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:43,000
Read 3: find ticket 1001, sort newest, limit 2

10
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:48,900
Three reads build the page: the cost is extra retrieval. History stays complete and queryable.

11
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:52,900
Contact correction: one person document changes. No ticket holds a copy to fix.

12
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:56,400
New event 5998: a new history document. The ticket does not grow.

13
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:00,100
The page now shows 5998 and 5002. 5001 is still stored: count = 3.

14
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:03,100
requester_id: 101 is not a foreign key. Nothing checks that person 101 exists.

15
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:06,100
Three reads are not one atomic snapshot. A change can land between them.

16
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:09,600
A hybrid (copy the latest two into the ticket) needs a refresh rule. Not automatically better.

17
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:21,200
Remember: Start from the reads, writes, growth, and owner of each fact. Embed what is read together, owned, and bounded. Reference what grows without bound or is shared and changes. A reference is not a foreign key; the app must keep it valid. Pick the simplest design that meets the stated workload.

